## 7. Security and governance

### Baseline security controls (MVP)

| Risk | Control |
|---|---|
| Malicious instructions inside issue text or artifacts | Treat task input and artifacts as untrusted data; system policy and Skills take precedence |
| Agent reads secrets | Environment allowlist, secrets redaction, protected paths (`.env`, credential files) |
| Dangerous CLI commands | Explicit command/tool policy; deny destructive infrastructure actions by default |
| Unauthorized PRs or deploys | Human approval required for external side effects |
| Shell injection via YAML | Validate schema; avoid executing unchecked strings as shell commands |
| Parallel file conflicts | Isolated worktrees; immutable validation snapshots |
| Runaway loops and spend | Max attempts, max stages, concurrency caps, timeouts, optional token budgets |
| Unclear attribution | Per-stage execution events, actor IDs, artifact hashes, and audit log |
| Incomplete failure recovery | Durable state, reconciliation, idempotency keys |

**Environment considerations:** Keep logs, artifacts, secrets, and any future telemetry exporters in destinations approved for the organization's boundary. A local prototype should not silently forward sensitive prompts or run contents to an external observability service.

### Example approval conditions

- Before creating or updating a GitHub PR.
- Before modifying protected branches or repository settings.
- Before running infrastructure apply/destroy commands.
- Before contacting a new network endpoint or accessing a new secret scope.

